cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use pilotr, please cite it as below."
title: "pilotr: Simulate experimental and behavioural data from a portable design specification"
abstract: "Generative simulation of experimental and behavioural data from a portable JSON design specification shared by a no-code app, an R package and a Python package, with simulation-based power and precision/ROPE design analysis and a shared cross-language random-number generator that makes the simulated data bit-identical across implementations."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bernabeu
    given-names: Pablo
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1083-2460"
    email: pcbernabeu@gmail.com
# Neither field is generated from anything, so both are set by hand at each release. The R
# and Python packages are released separately, and this file follows the newest release of
# either, with the date it first became public (on CRAN or PyPI). tools/check_versions.py
# fails CI when the version is not the newer of the two package versions.
# First public on CRAN, 2026-09-20 (R package, while PyPI stays at 0.3.0).
version: "0.3.1"
date-released: "2026-09-20"
# This file describes both packages, so its DOI is the Zenodo concept DOI, which archives
# every GitHub release of the repository and resolves to the latest.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21266313"
# Both DOIs, since doi holds one. The R package's own citation, in citation("pilotr") and
# on its website, gives the DOI CRAN assigned to it, which names the R package alone.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21266313"
    description: "Every GitHub release of the repository, with both packages, archived on Zenodo."
  - type: doi
    value: "10.32614/CRAN.package.pilotr"
    description: "The R package on CRAN, across all its versions."
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/pablobernabeu/pilotr"
url: "https://pablobernabeu.github.io/pilotr/"
keywords:
  - simulation
  - power analysis
  - mixed-effects models
  - experimental design
  - reproducible research
